WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.210
- A patient walks into my
office expecting a prescription

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:05.370
for an antibiotic but I don't write one.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:06.720
Let's talk about what happens

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:08.693
on this week's Wednesday Checkup.

00:00:08.693 --> 00:00:09.534
Bee-whoop!

00:00:09.534 --> 00:00:12.534
(smooth jazz music)

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:17.650
Let's set the scene here,

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:19.300
we have a 38 year old female

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:20.930
that comes in with a chief complaint

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:22.740
of upper respiratory symptoms.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.100
It's been going on for a
period of two to three days

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:28.170
and her main complaints come
from fatigue, sore throat,

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:29.850
dry cough and runny nose.

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:31.550
A little bit of background on the patient,

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:33.960
she has two children,
is currently married,

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:35.970
and has a very stressful job.

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:37.920
Probably the most important
part of this story

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.690
is that she has a vacation
coming up in 11 days

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:42.430
and she's worried that this infection

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:44.010
may interfere with those plans.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:46.360
Normal vital signs, no fever,

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:49.700
pert positives: inflamed
nostrils, inflamed throat.

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.570
Pert negatives: no exudates on the tonsils

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:54.400
which means those nasty little white

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.170
plaques that you sometimes see,

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:59.210
clear lungs, no abdominal pain, no rashes

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:02.240
and very importantly,
no swollen lymph nodes.

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:04.720
At this point, I had a
very strong suspicion

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:06.500
that my patient has a viral illness.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.080
I didn't even know which viral illness.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:09.680
But it doesn't make much of a difference

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:11.580
because it doesn't change my management.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:13.920
Viruses are treated in the same manner.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:15.970
We give supportive care to the patient,

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:18.680
treat their symptoms, make
sure they get enough rest,

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:21.040
hydration, in order for their own bodies

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:22.700
to fight off those infections.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:25.160
When I told the patient
about my diagnosis,

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:27.730
she met it with hesitation and wanted

00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:28.563
to know if she was going

00:01:28.563 --> 00:01:30.380
to get a prescription for antibiotics.

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.340
I explained to her that antibiotics

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:34.220
don't work on viral conditions,

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:36.140
like the one I suspect
that she was having.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:38.610
I had to explain to her
that weighing the risks

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:40.570
versus benefits here, I still thought

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:43.300
that not using antibiotics
was the right move.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:45.160
She put her trust in me and told me

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:46.510
that she would call me over the weekend

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:48.000
if any problems were to arise.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.499
Come Sunday, two days later,

00:01:49.499 --> 00:01:51.980
the patient calls my
office and lets me know

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:53.610
that she's not getting better

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:54.560
and she's wondering if she should

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:56.130
go to a urgent care facility.

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:57.714
I talked to her about her symptoms

00:01:57.714 --> 00:02:00.350
it just to me seems like her viral illness

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:02.570
still hasn't improved
because she hasn't reached

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:05.400
that day six, day seven where
we usually see a turn around.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.870
And I told her that it
was in her best interest

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:10.100
to stick with the symptoms management,

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:14.230
treat herself with rest,
hydration, with green tea

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:16.440
and honey and stick through it and give

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:17.560
me a call in two days.

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:20.460
Surprise, surprise, patient
calls me in two days,

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:22.360
let's me know that she's
feeling a lot better

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.130
and turned around on day 6 all

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:25.880
her symptoms pretty much resolved.

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:28.380
She did let me know that
on Sunday she ended up

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:30.270
going to an urgent care facility

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:32.290
and got a rapid strep test done

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:35.270
which came up negative
as we initially thought

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:37.620
so she was very grateful
that we didn't unnecessarily

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:40.170
treat her with antibiotics
and it was a good result.

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:41.730
And I thought it was
important to talk about

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:44.340
this result because it's
a very common situation

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:47.710
where doctors either
over-prescribe antibiotics

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:49.930
or doctors don't prescribe an antibiotic

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:51.670
and then patients get
really upset about it.

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:52.660
So let's talk about that.

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:54.560
The situation of a patient coming

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:58.260
in and requesting antibiotics
when their not actually needed

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:00.320
or indicated happens quite often.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:03.190
And I want to start off
telling you why doctors

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:06.440
should not be prescribing
antibiotics unnecessarily.

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:08.550
Reason number one: resistance.

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:11.330
Antibiotics work against certain bacteria

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:12.710
that are not resistant to them.

00:03:12.710 --> 00:03:15.354
However, when you give
patients antibiotics

00:03:15.354 --> 00:03:19.130
over and over again you
create a type of bacteria

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:22.370
known as a super bug which
actually becomes resistant

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:24.061
to that antibiotic and therefore

00:03:24.061 --> 00:03:26.350
that antibiotic becomes useless.

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:28.410
And there's only so many
antibiotics out there

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:29.910
where we can actually run

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:31.840
out of antibiotics to give a patient

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:33.770
because they have such a multi-drug

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:35.370
resistant bacterial infection.

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:37.510
Second: adverse reactions.

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:39.280
I'm talking about allergic reactions

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:42.480
where the throat closes
up, rashes, diarrhea

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.970
an infection known as
clostridium difficile,

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:47.840
where you kill all the good
bacteria in your bowels

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:50.720
so that this bad bacteria
grows and festers

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:52.600
and you have the worst bacteria ever.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:56.550
Three: Antibiotics don't
work against viruses

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:59.100
I know many people say "Well
as soon as I got the antibiotic

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:01.180
I felt better," you would have felt better

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:02.500
without the antibiotic.

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:05.867
In fact, when we treat a
strep throat with antibiotics,

00:04:05.867 --> 00:04:08.580
we're not even really
treating the strep throat.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:10.180
It would get better on it's own.

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:11.870
The reason we give antibiotics here

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:15.080
is to prevent the
complications of strep throat.

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:17.470
Like rheumatic fever, kidney issues.

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:18.880
Let that sink in for a moment.

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:21.400
And finally fourth: the cost.

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:23.670
Why would you spend money on antibiotics

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:26.340
when you have a virus, they're
not even gonna be helping you

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:27.690
and possibly harming you.

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:29.050
The art of medicine is about

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:32.470
balancing the risks versus
benefits to be in your favor.

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:34.150
Now let's move on and talk about

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:37.975
what pressures doctors face
to prescribe antibiotics

00:04:37.975 --> 00:04:39.770
even though they may not be

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:41.500
the appropriate choice of treatment.

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:43.490
First: Patient request.

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:45.930
Like we had in my story, patient comes in

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:48.390
and requests firmly an antibiotic.

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:50.500
You can discuss very clearly with them

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:53.510
why you think an antibiotic
isn't the optimal choice.

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:55.560
You can explain the risks, the benefits,

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:58.470
and the patient can still
request an antibiotic.

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:00.070
So, you go ahead and write one.

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:02.790
The second connects very
closely with the first,

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:04.970
and it's these doctor review sites like

00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:06.874
Zocdoc, Yelp and so forth.

00:05:06.874 --> 00:05:09.470
Where if a patient
doesn't get the treatment

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:10.660
they expected to get,

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:13.100
they can leave a nasty
review that can truly hurt

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:14.140
your own business.

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:16.160
Think about it, if I open my own practice,

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:19.060
and I'm trying to establish
myself and have a good name

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:20.650
and give quality service,

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:23.320
and not give unnecessary antibiotics,

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:24.830
but a patient really wanted them,

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:26.060
they leave me a bad review,

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:27.550
that can really hurt my business.

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:29.370
Three is something you
may not have heard of,

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:33.720
it's CYA medicine, Cover
Your "Butt" medicine.

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:36.010
Essentially what doctors
are doing, is that

00:05:36.010 --> 00:05:38.920
they're afraid that if you
don't prescribe antibiotics

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:40.330
for a patient, and they actually do

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:42.390
have a bacterial
infection, they get worse,

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:44.950
they may be held liable through a lawsuit,

00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:46.730
and that's scary stuff for a doctor.

00:05:46.730 --> 00:05:49.060
You don't wanna be sued by a patient,

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:50.940
especially in the age we live in

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:53.910
where it seems everybody's
just suing everybody

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:54.750
all the time.

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:56.967
Fourth is patient satisfaction.

00:05:56.967 --> 00:06:00.050
If a patient pays to go
to the doctor's visit

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:02.290
and leaves with no prescription,

00:06:02.290 --> 00:06:03.840
and instead leaves with advice

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:06.080
to drink green tea, honey and hydrate,

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:08.150
they feel like they didn't
get a lot out of the visit

00:06:08.150 --> 00:06:09.640
and can leave very unhappy.

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:11.810
But we have to remember, the reason we pay

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:15.210
for a doctor's visit is
not to buy a prescription,

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:18.410
it's to consult a doctor
on their medical opinion

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:20.130
as to what's going on with you

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:21.980
and what is the best treatment option

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:23.130
to solve that problem.

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:26.248
Please don't think I'm anti antibiotics.

00:06:26.248 --> 00:06:29.929
Antibiotics are a life saving
drug, and when appropriate

00:06:29.929 --> 00:06:31.890
is so important to give.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:34.600
In fact, if someone has a viral infection,

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:36.460
they become more susceptible to getting

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:38.620
a bacterial infection on top of it.

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:40.590
So that if you initially have symptoms

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:43.260
of a viral illness that
suddenly get worse,

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:44.850
you may need antibiotics.

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:46.960
I'm definitely for antibiotics,

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:49.850
but I'm for responsible
use of antibiotics.

00:06:49.850 --> 00:06:52.499
We covered antibiotics
and I told you my story,

00:06:52.499 --> 00:06:55.890
but I also happened to
go to my P.O. box today

00:06:55.890 --> 00:06:57.500
and picked up some packages (shaking box)

00:06:57.500 --> 00:06:58.700
Pennsylvania

00:06:58.700 --> 00:07:01.420
Oh my god why is everyone
sending these shirts?

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:03.560
It's a Newfoundland
with a little heartbeat.

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:05.106
Bear, what do you think about this shirt?

00:07:05.106 --> 00:07:07.260
(laughing) What do you think?

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:09.040
California, San Diego.

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:10.093
Oh my (laughing) god.

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:13.650
Chest compressions! Chest compressions!

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:15.887
Cynthia, thank you so much for this shirt.

00:07:15.887 --> 00:07:18.190
"If you don't start making
merch soon I swear to god."

00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:19.023
I'm sorry!

00:07:19.023 --> 00:07:20.870
I'm working with the trademark attorney,

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:22.676
there's so much nonsense when it comes

00:07:22.676 --> 00:07:24.280
to making your own merch.

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:26.345
I'm a doctor, I have
no idea what I'm doing,

00:07:26.345 --> 00:07:28.450
but I promise there's gonna be merch soon.

00:07:28.450 --> 00:07:29.283
Japan!

00:07:29.283 --> 00:07:32.351
(surprised) Ooh! A little
Japanese Kit-Kat action.

00:07:32.351 --> 00:07:35.430
Kit-Kat Easter eggs,
white chocolate Kit-Kats.

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:36.892
Thank you my Japanese friend.

00:07:36.892 --> 00:07:39.019
This is from Denmark, look at this.

00:07:39.019 --> 00:07:40.850
Look at these candies.

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:42.240
Look at these candies!

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:44.100
This is what's leading
the obesity epidemic

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:44.933
but I'm gonna love it.

00:07:44.933 --> 00:07:45.766
Thank you so much.

00:07:45.766 --> 00:07:47.320
"Safe guard your families health

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:48.461
with a mild laxative.

00:07:48.461 --> 00:07:50.785
Healthaway products from 195-"

00:07:50.785 --> 00:07:54.500
(gasp) Is this like, this looks genuine.

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:56.253
This is like an artifact.

00:07:56.253 --> 00:07:58.010
Carol, this is amazing!

00:07:58.010 --> 00:08:00.010
I'm gonna save this, I don't
even wanna show you guys

00:08:00.010 --> 00:08:00.843
what this is.

00:08:00.843 --> 00:08:02.610
This is weird health
products from the 50's.

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:03.880
This one's from Jamaica.

00:08:03.880 --> 00:08:05.840
Ooh! "Our Daily Bread"

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:07.284
because everybody needs a carb.

00:08:07.284 --> 00:08:08.481
Bear, bomb check?

00:08:08.481 --> 00:08:09.367
Good.

00:08:09.367 --> 00:08:13.230
"First aid kit, do not
open unless emergency"

00:08:13.230 --> 00:08:14.063
That's so sweet.

00:08:14.063 --> 00:08:17.980
You guys need to watch my
video that I did with my nephew

00:08:17.980 --> 00:08:21.210
Ari where we did a Disgusting
Science Experiments,

00:08:21.210 --> 00:08:23.090
so click here for that bad boy.

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:25.823
And as always, stay happy and healthy.

00:08:27.432 --> 00:08:28.490
(laugh)

